WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:17.970
For the sixth day of Christmas we've gone
with carbon, element number six and a

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:24.689
particular sort of coal, called anthracite, which
is almost pure carbon. This is a sample

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:30.900
of anthracite that was sent to me by a
YouTube fan called David Harris; I think in

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.730
Pennsylvania. Anthracite is really, looks
rather beautiful, it looks almost like

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:44.290
bits of metal. It's very light and it's
almost pure carbon. I think after

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:51.390
Christmas we're going to get Neil to burn some
of this, but David also sent me some ash

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:56.739
that is left over from the burning. Some
of it went into the bottle, I put it into

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:02.820
a bottle, but one piece was just too big
and what's interesting about this ash.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:08.410
which I think was burnt in a furnace,
is that it's very light. I think these

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:13.890
are just bits of minerals which were
included in the lumps of anthracite

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.810
and they didn't remove properly. According to David that anthracite

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.990
with almost a colorless flame. So again,
that's something which we can test in

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:31.270
a future video. And although it's coal,
it's not at all dirty. I can rub it on my

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:39.340
hand and you can see none of it comes
off. So, for the sixth day of Christmas

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.650
element carbon in anthracite, a glowing guitar plectrum,

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:52.800
four drinks containers, three chemical badges, two periodic table bed covers,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:00.260
one piece of tartaric acid from a Swiss wine barrel.
And let's see what's number seven.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.940
[Prof. Licence] ...gas we get out into our pipe.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.020
So this is really for safety because the pressure inside the cylinder,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.920
is a lot higher than the pressure that we
require

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.040
in our reaction or inside our balloon.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.920
[Prof. Poliakoff] So this is my present for the first day of Christmas.

